  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved Enabling environment to facilitate the integration of SMEs and Cooperatives into local and global value chains</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Mozambique&#x2019;s Economic growth has not resulted in equitable human development. The poverty rate is estimated to have increased owing to loss of income and employment, pushing an estimated additional one million people into poverty. Challenges affecting Small and Medium Enterprises in Mozambique include poor trade, financing, and investment conditions. Most are informal and cannot meet financial institutions requirements, including collaterals, audited accounts and business plans. In achieving sustainable development, human well-being and the environment are strongly inter-linked. Ecosystems provide natural resources, materials and services upon which human life is sustained. People depend on natural resources for their basic needs such as food, water, air, energy and housing. Environmental changes and impacts on human well-being are induced by various drivers and pressures, among them demographic changes, trade, science and technology. Mozambique is endowed with an abundance of diverse minerals and other natural resources. In several of the economic sectors, especially mining and housing, much of the positive economic performance has been characterised by negative drivers, pressures and impacts on the environment such as increased natural resource exploitation, urbanisation, pollution and changing consumer patterns, notably energy consumption. 

Partners include SMES, Business Development Service Providers, Government line ministry in charge of Commerce and National Employment Institute (INEP).</narrative>
  </description>
